WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:01.990
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.990 --> 00:00:04.060
How many friends do you have?

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:11.659
If the answer is one or two, or even none
at all, then you could be an extraordinary

00:00:11.659 --> 00:00:14.999
intelligent person, possibly even genius level.

00:00:14.999 --> 00:00:18.610
Either that or you’re a massive gentleman’s
pickle.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:23.290
Psychologist Satoshi Kanazawa of the London
School of Economics measured the happiness

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:27.080
levels of 15,000 people with various IQ levels.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:31.310
He measured the happiness of all the subjects
on two occasions, once whilst they were socialising

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:35.699
with a group of friends and then again when
they were completely alone.

00:00:35.699 --> 00:00:43.069
What the results showed was that for people
with low to average intelligence levels they

00:00:43.069 --> 00:00:49.090
reported greater self-happiness when they
spent time with friends and it seems the more

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:52.539
friends they had, the happier it made them.

00:00:52.539 --> 00:00:58.120
But astonishingly, the exact opposite was
true for people with very high intelligence

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:02.319
levels – they reported greater happiness
when alone.

00:01:02.319 --> 00:01:07.140
In fact it was noted that those at the top
end of the intelligence spectrum actually

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:12.200
felt considerably less happy when spending
time with friends.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.640
Some theorise this is because intelligent
individuals are more sceptical of others,

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:22.180
they are more-able to see the faults inherent
in humankind and so prefer not to get tangled

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:26.190
up in the blues of their unpredictable and
unreliable brethren.

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:31.490
It’s also thought that when intelligent
people do form friendships, it’s mostly

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:36.610
for personal gain or simply out of awkwardness
and/or expectancy.

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:43.620
Whereas most other people form friendships
for emotional support or a lust for attention

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.110
and popularity.

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:50.730
Smart people don’t need emotional support;
we have text books to cry into.

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:57.620
This aversion towards human interaction actually
intensifies in a predictable and linear manner

00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:59.890
with increased intelligence.

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:04.660
In the most prime examples of mental acuity
that we’ve come to know and learn about

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:11.550
in school, we see a great struggle to form
bonds and relationships with other humans.

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:18.840
Nikola Tesla never married or had sex, his
tastes were far more avian in nature – he

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:21.170
fell in love with a pigeon…

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:23.590
I shit you not.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:30.340
He grew a great fondness for one particular
pigeon and actually wrote this about her “I

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:34.700
loved that pigeon as a man loves a woman,
and she loved me.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.819
As long as I had her, there was a purpose
to my life.”

00:02:37.819 --> 00:02:40.580
…That’s a bit weird mate.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:46.270
Brian May lookalike and physicist Isaac Newton
was one of the most brilliant men to ever

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:47.740
walk the Earth.

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:52.959
Seriously we owe a lot to him, he founded
gravity, calculus and the laws of motion,

00:02:52.959 --> 00:02:58.459
not to mention countless others – he basically
created the groundwork for all modern-day

00:02:58.459 --> 00:02:59.459
physics.

00:02:59.459 --> 00:03:05.190
Yet, to say he was socially-awkward is a gross
understatement.

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:10.709
He was immensely private, never formed any
kind of friendship, apart from a mild acquaintanceship

00:03:10.709 --> 00:03:14.819
with a Swiss mathematician and he died a virgin.

00:03:14.819 --> 00:03:21.680
“Happiness in intelligent people is the
rarest thing I know” – Earnest Hemingway;

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:23.640
and he met a LOT of people.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:28.720
It’s not that highly intelligent people
are anti-social it’s just that they are

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:34.870
in the vast minority and so struggle to find
others that share their views on the world,

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:39.890
and accept them for all their faults and often
acutely-fastidious nature.

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:45.060
Because after all, no matter how many times
you’ve been told the scientifically-proven

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:50.220
falsehood of “opposites attract”, it’s
similarities that bring people together and

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:52.300
form lasting bonds.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:57.739
And I guess similarities with others are scarce
when you are adamant that the Earth revolves

00:03:57.739 --> 00:04:02.069
around the Sun and the rest of the moronic
folk you share the planet with point-blankly

00:04:02.069 --> 00:04:03.410
refuse to believe you.

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:08.599
I know Copernicus, it’s tough, but hang
in there, they’ll believe you, eventually

00:04:08.599 --> 00:04:11.700
– just not in your lifetime.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:17.160
Kanazawa’s study also found a disturbing
correlation that affects all of us, not just

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:18.449
the geeks.

00:04:18.449 --> 00:04:23.900
His findings showed that people who lived
in more densely populated areas were significantly

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:28.170
less happy than those in less populated areas.

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:32.140
This is not the first scientific study to
acknowledge this phenomenon.

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:37.420
Countless studies have found that big cities
harbour hatred towards our fellow people.

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:41.930
Whereas the friendliest and most inviting
of people can be found in small towns and

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:43.440
rural settlements.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.260
This phenomenon has been observed all over
the world.

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:50.830
Heck you don’t even need studies to observe
this – just try making eye contact with

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:58.310
literally anybody in London, or just being
a person in Paris – people can be frosty

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:00.000
at the best of times.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.190
Even in these world-class cities that supposedly
provide everything a person could ever want

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:06.680
and more.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:11.180
Does this reveal a dark truth about the human
condition?

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:16.410
Perhaps we’re not designed to live together
with one-another, no matter how intelligent

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:18.410
you may be.

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:25.200
But that’s not it, because studies show
that friendliness and compassion thrive in

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:27.990
very small communities.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:31.510
It actually goes back to our evolutionary
past.

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:37.520
Our ancestors living on the great planes of
Africa would have lived in small groups, it

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:41.980
was just more practical this way – easier
to travel and safer when you know everyone

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:51.030
around you by grunt.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:55.610
Groups rarely mixed and if they did it would
probably end up in conflict, so people just

00:05:55.610 --> 00:06:00.120
stayed in their small communities, hundreds
of miles from each other.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:07.070
And that same attitude is still instilled
deep within us today, as much as some people

00:06:07.070 --> 00:06:11.640
feel like moving to a big city and forming
an enormous circle of friends will bring them

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:19.419
success and happiness, we are just fighting
against our own evolution.

00:06:19.419 --> 00:06:23.220
Perhaps we should all learn to get along with
the people we know first.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:29.070
Unless you’re highly intelligent, in which
case, I’m sorry but you’re just not cut-out

00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:38.050
for relationships, maybe try befriending pigeon,
who knows, it could even lead to more.

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:48.240
Thanks for watching.

